1. Create a new Active Directory domain called 'musicstream.local' for a fictional music streaming startup, and add five user accounts representing employees from different departments (e.g., admin, marketing, tech, support, HR).
=>Open Server Manager → Add Roles and Features.
Select Role-based or feature-based installation → choose the local server.
Install the Active Directory Domain Services role (include management tools). Restart if prompted.
After installation, click the notification flag → Promote this server to a domain controller.
Choose Add a new forest.
Root domain name: musicstream.local.
Set a strong Directory Services Restore Mode (DSRM) password and complete the wizard (defaults are fine for a lab). The server will reboot and become the domain controller.
Log in as musicstream\Administrator.
Open Active Directory Users and Computers (dsa.msc).
Expand musicstream.local → right-click Users (or create an OU called “Employees”) → New → User. Create these five accounts

2. Configure DNS and DHCP services on your Windows Server so that all devices in the 'musicstream.local' network automatically receive IP addresses and can resolve domain names within the network.<br><br><em><strong>Hint:</strong> Use the Server Manager to install and configure both roles, and test by connecting a client VM or device.</em>
=>DNS (already installed with AD DS):

DNS is automatically configured for the new domain.
Open DNS Manager (dnsmgmt.msc) and confirm the forward lookup zone musicstream.local exists and contains the domain controller’s records.
Create a reverse lookup zone if desired (optional for a lab).

DHCP:

In Server Manager → Add Roles and Features → install the DHCP Server role.
Complete the DHCP post-deployment configuration wizard (authorize the DHCP server in AD).
Open DHCP console (dhcpmgmt.msc).
Right-click IPv4 → New Scope.
Name the scope (e.g., “MusicStream LAN”).
Define an IP range (example: 192.168.10.100 – 192.168.10.200), subnet mask 255.255.255.0.
Exclude any static addresses you need.
Set lease duration (e.g., 8 days).
Configure options:
Router (default gateway) – your server’s IP or the lab gateway.
DNS server – the domain controller’s IP address.
DNS domain name – musicstream.local.

Activate the scope.

3. Set up a shared folder named 'PlaylistShares' on your server, give read/write access only to the 'marketing' and 'tech' users, and map it as a network drive on their accounts.
=>On the server create a folder, e.g. C:\Shares\PlaylistShares.
Right-click the folder → Properties → Sharing → Advanced Sharing.
Share the folder as PlaylistShares.
Permissions: remove Everyone; add marketing and tech with Change (or Full Control if preferred).

On the Security tab (NTFS permissions):
Remove overly broad inherited permissions if needed (or disable inheritance carefully).
Give marketing and tech Modify (or Read & Write) permissions.
Leave Administrators / SYSTEM with Full Control.

Map the drive for the users:
Log on as marketing or tech (on a domain-joined client).
File Explorer → This PC → Map network drive → choose a letter (e.g. P:) → folder \\dc.musicstream.local\PlaylistShares (or \\servername\PlaylistShares) → Reconnect at sign-in.
Alternatively, use Group Policy Preferences or a logon script for automatic mapping.

4. Create a Group Policy Object (GPO) that enforces password complexity and disables USB storage for all users except the 'admin' account.
=>Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc).
Expand Forest → Domains → musicstream.local.
Right-click the domain (or an OU that contains the users) → Create a GPO in this domain, and Link it here. Name it e.g. “Security – Password & USB”.
Edit the GPO:
Computer Configuration → Policies → Windows Settings → Security Settings → Account Policies → Password Policy
Password must meet complexity requirements → Enabled
(Optionally set Minimum password length, history, etc.)

Computer Configuration → Policies → Administrative Templates → System → Removable Storage Access
All Removable Storage classes: Deny all access → Enabled
(Or specifically “Removable Disks: Deny execute/read/write access”)


To exempt the admin account:
Best practice: place the admin account in a separate OU that does not have this GPO linked, or
Use Security Filtering on the GPO: remove “Authenticated Users”, add a security group that contains everyone except admin, or
Use Item-level targeting / WMI filtering, or simply apply the USB restriction only to a “Users” OU that excludes Domain Admins.

Force update on clients: gpupdate /force.

5. Write a step-by-step guide (max 1 page) explaining how to restore a deleted user account from backup in your 'musicstream.local' domain, including the tools and commands you would use.<br><br><em><strong>Hint:</strong> Mention Windows Server Backup and Active Directory Users and Computers.</em>
=>Boot the domain controller into Directory Services Restore Mode (DSRM) – press F8 during startup or use msconfig / recovery options and select Safe Mode / DSRM. Log in with the DSRM password set during promotion.
Open Windows Server Backup → Recover.
Select the backup that contains System State → choose System State recovery → select Original location → enable Perform an authoritative restore of Active Directory files.
After the System State restore completes, open an elevated command prompt before rebooting and run:cmdntdsutil
activate instance ntds
authoritative restore
restore object "CN=Username,OU=Employees,DC=musicstream,DC=local"(or restore subtree for an entire OU).
Type quit twice, then reboot normally.
After reboot, open Active Directory Users and Computers, confirm the user is restored, reset the password if necessary, and re-add the account to any groups that were lost.

